| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **Map stays blank after the missing-icon request** | You forgot to call `map.addImage()` *inside* the resolver, or you called it after the resolver returned. | `addImage` must be called **before** the resolver resolves. Use `await` on any async work, then call `addImage`. |
| **`styleimagemissing` fires repeatedly** | The resolver never adds the image (or adds it with a different id). | Verify that the `id` you receive matches exactly the one you pass to `addImage`. |
| **Image appears but is blurry** | The image’s pixel ratio is wrong. | Pass `{ pixelRatio: window.devicePixelRatio }` (or `2` for retina) as the third argument to `addImage`. |
| **Using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.) and the map never renders** | The worker URL isn’t set. | Call `setWorkerUrl()` **once** before creating the map (see migration guide). |
| **I’m still using `require('maplibre-gl')`** | v6 removed the CommonJS entry point. | Convert the file to ESM (`import …`) or load dynamically with `await import('maplibre-gl')`. |
| **I want to keep the old `styleimagemissing` listener for logging** | It still works, but it won’t resolve the request. | Keep it; just remember it’s “notify-only”. The resolver does the actual work. |

---

## 6️⃣ Full migration checklist (if you’re coming from v5)

1. **Switch to the ESM build** (`dist/maplibre-gl.mjs` or `import … from 'maplibre-gl'`).  
2. **Remove default import** – use `import * as maplibregl` or named imports.  
3. **Replace any `require('maplibre-gl')`** with an ESM import or dynamic `await import()`.  
4. **Replace `styleimagemissing`-addImage pattern** with `setMissingStyleImageResolver`.  
5. **If you accessed `map.transform`**, migrate to the public camera getters (`getZoom()`, `getCenter()`, …).  
6. **Update TypeScript event types** – use `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent` instead of `MapDataEvent`.  
7. **If you bundle**, add a one-time `setWorkerUrl()` call (see the migration guide for the exact snippet for your bundler).  

Once those steps are done, the dynamic-icon workflow above will work without any further changes.

---

### TL;DR code snippet you can copy-paste

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({ container: 'map', style: 'style.json' });

map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // Example: fetch PNG from a CDN
  const resp = await fetch(`https://my.cdn/icons/${id}.png`);
  const blob = await resp.blob();
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(blob);
  map.addImage(id, bitmap);
});
```

That’s it – the map will now automatically request the missing icon, you’ll fetch/generate it, add it to the map, and the style will render the feature as if the sprite had contained the image all along. Happy mapping!

Below is a **minimal, working example** that shows how to bootstrap a MapLibre GL JS map in a **Vite + TypeScript** project that you installed from npm (`npm i maplibre-gl`).  
All of the v5-→-v6 breaking-changes that affect a Vite build are handled:

* **ESM-only import** – no default export.  
* **`setWorkerUrl()`** – required for bundled builds (Vite’s `?worker&url` helper).  
* **`styleimagemissing`** → `setMissingStyleImageResolver`.  
* **No `map.transform`** – use the public camera getters.  
* **Typed `data` events** – `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent`.

You can copy-paste the code into a fresh Vite project (or drop it into an existing one) and it will just work.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the dependencies

```bash
# From the root of your Vite project
npm i maplibre-gl
# (optional, but recommended) install the types for the DOM if you don’t already have them
npm i -D @types/dom
```

> **Why not `mapbox-gl`?**  
> MapLibre GL JS v6 removed the default export and the CommonJS entry point, so the only way to import it in a Vite/E​SM project is a **named or namespace import** (`import * as maplibregl …` or `import { Map } …`).  

---

## 2️⃣ Vite config (only if you want to tweak the worker handling)

Vite already knows how to treat `?worker&url` imports, so **no extra config is required**.  
If you have a custom `vite.config.ts` that changes the default asset handling, just make sure the `assetsInclude` pattern still matches `*.mjs`.

```ts
// vite.config.ts (optional – only needed if you have a non-default config)
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'; // or any other framework plugin

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()],
  // make sure .mjs files are treated as assets (default behaviour)
  assetsInclude: ['**/*.mjs'],
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ The map-initialisation code

Below is a **plain TypeScript module** (`src/map.ts`) that you can import from any UI framework (React, Vue, Svelte, plain HTML, …).  
All the v6-specific steps are highlighted with comments.

```ts
// src/map.ts
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';               // <-- bring in the default CSS
import {
  Map,
  NavigationControl,
  setWorkerUrl,
  // optional: you can also import other symbols you need, e.g. Marker, Popup …
} from 'maplibre-gl';

// Vite’s special import that gives us the *URL* of the worker file.
// The `?worker&url` query tells Vite to bundle the worker as a separate
// asset and return its final public URL.
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Tell MapLibre where the worker lives (required for any bundler build)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Optional: resolve missing style images at runtime
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// In v6 `styleimagemissing` is only a notification.  Use the resolver
// to add the image yourself.
export function registerMissingImageResolver(map: Map) {
  map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id: string) => {
    // Example: generate a simple SVG data-URL on the fly.
    const svg = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="64" height="64">
                   <circle cx="32" cy="32" r="30" fill="orange"/>
                 </svg>`;
    const blob = new Blob([svg], { type: 'image/svg+xml' });
    const img = await createImageBitmap(blob);
    map.addImage(id, img);
  });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Create the map instance
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
export function createMap(containerId: string): Map {
  const map = new Map({
    container: containerId,               // id of the <div> in your HTML
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center: [0, 0],                       // [lng, lat]
    zoom: 2,
    // You can add any other Map options here (bearing, pitch, hash, …)
  });

  // Add the default navigation UI (zoom + compass)
  map.addControl(new NavigationControl());

  // Register the missing-image resolver (optional but often useful)
  registerMissingImageResolver(map);

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Example of using the new public camera API (instead of map.transform)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    console.log('Map loaded');
    console.log('Zoom:', map.getZoom());
    console.log('Center:', map.getCenter().toArray());
    console.log('Bearing:', map.getBearing());
    console.log('Pitch:', map.getPitch());
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ Typed data-event handling (MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('data', (e) => {
    // e is now a union type; narrow it to know which kind you have
    if ('sourceId' in e) {
      // ── MapSourceDataEvent ──
      console.log('Source data event', e.sourceId, e.sourceDataType);
    } else {
      // ── MapStyleDataEvent ──
      console.log('Style data event');
    }
  });

  return map;
}
```

### How to use it in a simple HTML page (no framework)

```html
<!-- public/index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Vite + MapLibre GL JS (v6)</title>
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; inset: 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
  <script type="module">
    import { createMap } from './src/map.ts';
    // The map will be created as soon as the module loads
    createMap('map');
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Note:** Because the page is served by Vite’s dev server, the `type="module"` script works out-of-the-box. In production Vite will bundle everything, and the `setWorkerUrl()` call will still point at the correct worker file.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist – “Did I miss any v6 gotcha?”

| ✅ Feature | What you need to do in Vite |
|------------|-----------------------------|
| **ESM-only import** | `import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl'` (no default export) |
| **CommonJS `require()`** | Not supported – convert to ESM or use dynamic `await import()` |
| **Worker URL** | `setWorkerUrl(workerUrl)` with the `?worker&url` import |
| **Missing style images** | Use `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()` (the old `styleimagemissing` handler no longer resolves) |
| **`map.transform`** | Replace with `map.getZoom()`, `map.getCenter()`, `map.getBearing()`, `map.getPitch()` |
| **`MapDataEvent`** | Type the handler as `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent` and narrow with `'sourceId' in e` |
| **CSS** | Import `maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css` (or copy it into your own stylesheet) |
| **Pin the version** | In `package.json` use `"maplibre-gl": "^6.0.0"` (or a concrete `6.x.y`) – never `latest` on a CDN because the next major will break the import path. |

---

